# Statistical Analysis

Study characteristics and outcome rates were first summarized descriptively. Continuous variables are reported as mean (SD), median [Q1, Q3] and range; categorical variables as counts and percentages.\
For each outcome (anastomotic leak, overall or severe postoperative complications, mortality), event counts and sample sizes were analyzed using a generalized linear mixed-effects model (GLMM) with a binomial likelihood, logit link, and random study-level intercept [Schwarzer, Carpenter, and Rücker, 2015].\
A random-effects model was used throughout, with $\tau^2$ estimated by maximum likelihood [Viechtbauer, 2005]. Confidence intervals were based on the Knapp-Hartung approach for standard random-effects meta-analysis [Knapp and Hartung, 2003]. Heterogeneity was reported as Cochran's $Q$, $I^2$ (with 95% CI) and $\tau^2$ [Higgins and Thompson, 2002], together with a 95% prediction interval for each pooled estimate, calculated with the Higgins-Thompson-Spiegelhalter method [Higgins, Thompson, and Spiegelhalter, 2009].\
For the anastomotic leak outcome, prespecified subgroup analyses were run by stoma type (ileostomy vs. combined ileostomy/colostomy; colostomy alone included only two studies and was not analyzed separately), study design (retrospective cohort, prospective cohort, RCT) and publication period (<2010, 2010-2019, ≥2020). Each subgroup was pooled with the same random-effects GLMM, $\tau^2$ was estimated separately per subgroup, and differences between subgroups were assessed using a $Q$-test for subgroup differences [Borenstein and Higgins, 2013].\
Small-study effects were assessed visually using funnel plots and formally using Peters' regression test [Peters et al., 2006]. As a sensitivity analysis, the Duval and Tweedie trim-and-fill method [Duval and Tweedie, 2000] was used to estimate the pooled effect after imputing studies that would restore funnel-plot symmetry; given the substantial heterogeneity, these results were interpreted cautiously. The GLMM estimate was compared with a conventional inverse-variance random-effects meta-analysis of logit-transformed proportions.\
All analyses were performed in R version 4.6.1, using RStudio version 2026.07.1, with the **meta** [Balduzzi, Rücker, and Schwarzer, 2019], **metafor** [Viechtbauer, 2010] and **dmetar** [Harrer et al., 2021] packages.

